Trimmed real data (ruling T1), from Arch Linux pkgstats
(https://pkgstats.archlinux.de/api/packages), fetched on 2026-09-26.

The rows are real monthly counts for a few packages, left unchanged: count
(reports that month that include the package) and samples (the month's
largest count). 37 package-months in all:

- previous/ (a snapshot as a run on 2026-08-03 leaves it): ttf-jetbrains-mono
  and glibc for 2025-08 to 2026-07, and otf-departure-mono-nerd for the months
  it is listed (2026-05 to 2026-07);
- http/page-2026-08.json: 10 packages of August 2026;
- snapshot/: the 2026-09-26 run, which copies 2025-09 to 2026-07 from
  previous/ and fetches 2026-08.

Each month's list was cut to these packages, so the answer's "total" and
"count" are the number of rows kept (the real August 2026 list has 34,451
packages), and "popularity" was recomputed as count / samples x 100 rounded
to two decimals, as the server does. The manifests were written by running the
collector against these pages. No personal data: pkgstats publishes only
aggregate counts.

pkgstats states no license for its data. The site's code
(https://github.com/archlinux-de/pkgstats.archlinux.de) is GPL-3.0. Its privacy
policy says: "The data send by the pkgstats command line tool is stored
anonymously on the server."
